# Fleet — remote management and automated deployment

A built-in app, served at `/fleet` in the console. Modules:
[`fleet.py`](../../src/apps/fleet.py) (protocol + both roles),
[`fleet_state.py`](../../src/apps/fleet_state.py) (the trust ledger),
[`fleet_host.py`](../../src/apps/fleet_host.py) (machine facts + the update
plan), [`fleet_ssh.py`](../../src/apps/fleet_ssh.py) (LAN scan + SSH),
[`fleet_provision.py`](../../src/apps/fleet_provision.py) (bootstrap),
[`fleet_web.py`](../../src/apps/fleet_web.py) (the console bridge).

> **Off by default.** It can open a shell: we do not enable it just because it
> shipped. `--fleet` at launch, or the **Enable** button in the console's Apps
> section. See [`Docs/AppStore/guide`](../AppStore/guide) for deployed apps, and
> below for installing/uninstalling a **built-in** app.

## The two roles

Every node runs the same app and can play both roles:

- **agent** — it obeys: answers status, runs its own package manager, opens a
  shell, scans its LAN, provisions machines it can reach. Only for an operator
  **it has enrolled**, and only for the **capabilities** that enrolment
  granted;
- **operator** — it drives: asks nodes to enrol it, keeps the list of those that
  accepted, sends them commands.

## Authorisation — three independent gates

A command runs only if all three hold. They are independent on purpose: one
giving way is not enough.

1. **The mesh has authenticated the sender.** The DATA payload carries a
   `src_id` proven by the E2E session (ML-DSA + certificate chain).
2. **They are enrolled here, with this capability.** The ledger is local,
   persistent, and grows only by an explicit local decision.
3. **The command carries a fresh signature over its own bytes.** Every request
   embeds an app-auth assertion (see [`Docs/AppAuth/guide`](../AppAuth/guide))
   whose audience is **this** node, whose `purpose` names the capability, and
   whose `ctx` is the hash of the request body. A command replayed, redirected
   or altered does not verify.

Gate 3 is defence in depth over gate 1, but it brings what gate 1 cannot: the
proof is **portable**. "This node authorised exactly this command, at this
instant" stays verifiable once the session is gone — which is what makes a
remote-execution feature **auditable** and not merely functional.

> **The accepted exception: the shell.** Signing every keystroke would cost one
> post-quantum signature per character. **Opening** a shell is signed; the
> keystrokes that follow are bound to the `sid` returned and travel over the
> same end-to-end encrypted channel, from the same authenticated peer. The
> signed act is "open a shell in my name", which is the act that matters. Input
> naming a `sid` from **another** node is dropped.

## Capabilities

From the most harmless to total. One never implies another.

| Capability | What it allows |
|---|---|
| `status` | reading uptime, load, memory, disks |
| `update` | running the machine's package manager upgrade |
| `scan` | scanning this machine's LAN for SSH hosts |
| `provision` | installing NMesh on machines of that LAN |
| `shell` | opening an interactive shell as the node's user |
| `manage` | driving this node's **web console** remotely — **the target's console password is still required** |

## Enrolment — the notification

1. On the operator's PC: the **Nodes** section, the **Request access to a node**
   button, paste the node id and tick the capabilities.
2. On the target node: a **"Requests to manage this node"** strip appears at the
   top of `/fleet`. Somebody has to answer.
3. **Review & accept** opens the list of capabilities asked for. It can
   **narrow**, never widen: approving cannot grant more than the request
   displayed.
4. On acceptance the operator receives a **signed grant** it keeps — proof that
   this node really consented, verifiable months later.

Nothing is granted before that human answer. A repeated request **refreshes**
the entry instead of stacking up, and the queue is capped: a peer cannot drown
the notification.

`Revoke` cuts the relationship both ways, tells the other end, and closes the
shells opened by that peer.

## `manage` — driving another node's console

The operator opens **their own** console, picks a node in a context selector,
and the whole console starts talking about that node: overview, peers,
transports, apps, configuration, trace, password. One interface, two machines.

### Two keys, two people

This is the property that makes it acceptable:

| Key | Held by | What it opens |
|---|---|---|
| the `manage` grant | a human **on the target node** (they accept the request) | the channel |
| the target's console password | the operator | the session |

Neither on its own opens anything. A compromised operator holding the grant has
nothing until they know the password; someone who knows the password has nothing
until a node has granted them `manage`.

The password is typed once, travels **only** inside the mesh's E2E session, and
is neither stored nor logged. The token the remote console returns stays **in
the local console's memory**, indexed by the local session that opened it: the
browser never sees it, and signing out locally closes every remote session
opened from there.

### The relay reinvents nothing

The target node does not answer by itself: it **replays the call against its own
console**, over HTTP on loopback, exactly as a browser on that machine would
(`src/apps/fleet_console.py`). So the session check, the body caps, **the
login's anti-bruteforce lockout**, every route and every refusal are the ones
already written and already tested. The app is a pipe, not an authority. The
console's self-signed certificate is **pinned** by fingerprint: a process
squatting the port cannot answer in its place.

### What does not go through the relay

| Refused | Why |
|---|---|
| `/api/fleet/*` | a managed node is not a **bounce**. Without this, a grant would propagate to every node *that one* manages |
| `/api/remote/*` | the relay driving the relay — a loop with a mesh hop in it |
| `/api/chat/*` | somebody else's conversations are no part of managing their machine, and the grant never asked for that |
| anything that is not `/api/` | the page is already served locally; there is nothing to fetch over there |

Methods: `GET` and `POST` only.

### Bounds

| Bound | Value | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| a relayed request's body | 24 KiB (one frame) | beyond that, a clear message: do it from the node itself |
| a reassembled response | 512 KiB | on both sides; beyond that, an explicit error, **never** a truncated response |
| splitting a response | 32 KiB per frame | a busy node's snapshot does not fit in one frame |
| concurrent hosted calls | 8 per peer | a peer cannot open an unbounded number of local sockets |
| the duration of a call | 25 s | a stuck call must not hold a mesh handler |
| remote sessions | 8 per local session, 1 h of inactivity | bounded memory, automatic forgetting |

### In the interface

The selector appears only if the fleet app is running **and** at least one node
has granted `manage`. Once connected, a **warning strip** and the rail's mark
change colour: "which machine am I on" must never be a guess when every
destructive button on the page points elsewhere.

## Changing the rights of an existing relationship

Rights are not frozen at enrolment. Two moves, **deliberately asymmetric**:
handing a right back concerns only whoever holds it, granting one commits the
machine that bears it.

### The "Who controls this node" view

The **Who controls this node** section on `/fleet`: the list of nodes that can
act on this machine, with their capabilities as checkboxes. **Apply rights**
applies the ticked box immediately — additions included. That is legitimate and
it is the only place a right is added: the person ticking is on the machine that
carries the consequence, and already holds the authority the right delegates.
Unticking everything amounts to `Cut off` (a full revocation).

The operator is told by an `ENROL_GRANT` restating the grant, so that their own
list stops showing buttons that would now be refused — and starts showing one
that would not. That grant is accepted on their side only for the entry of the
node that signed it.

### Asking for one more right (the **Rights** button, operator side)

Ticking a capability you do not have sends an `ENROL_REQUEST` carrying **the
complete set** you want. On the target side that is exactly the enrolment
notification: parked for a human, never auto-accepted, and the approval can only
**narrow** what was asked. The strip shows "More rights requested" along with
what the requester already holds, so the delta is readable.

There is **no path** that widens a grant without a human on the target machine.
That is the property that prevents escalation: otherwise the weakest capability
would be enough to reach the others.

### Handing a right back (`ENROL_NARROW`, 0x06)

Unticking a capability you hold removes it **at once**, asking nobody's
permission: nobody needs to approve you holding *less*. The effect is local
first (a node that never hears us still leaves us with less), then announced by
a signed `ENROL_NARROW`.

On the target side, this frame is the only one accepted with no human, and it is
because it is **intersected** with what the sender already holds: at worst, a
forged or replayed frame removes rights from its own sender. Handing back the
last right ends the relationship. Losing `shell` closes the shells already open
— otherwise revoking would only stop the *next* one.

An unknown capability name is **not** read as "no rights": the request is
refused. Otherwise a typo would become a cut-off.

## `update` and `shell`: two routes to root, deliberately different

There is **no** "root" capability. There are two capabilities whose security
properties have nothing in common, and that is on purpose.

### `update` — unattended, therefore narrow

Updating needs root **with no human in front**. Giving the node's account a
general sudo rule for it would mean any bug in the process is a root bug — the
same bug that otherwise reaches an unprivileged account and stops there.

The right granted is therefore **a script**, and the sudoers rule names only
that path, with no arguments:

```
nmesh ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/lib/nmesh/nmesh-update
```

Two details do all the work:

- the script lives under `/usr/local/lib`, **not in the installation prefix** —
  that one belongs to the node's account, which could otherwise rewrite what it
  is allowed to run as root;
- it **refuses every argument** and runs a fixed sequence (the detected package
  manager's plan): nothing the node sends influences it, not a package name, not
  a flag, not a shell string.

Granted with `./install.sh --allow-update`, removed by running it again without
the flag — which **also rewrites the systemd unit**, because a unit hardened
with `NoNewPrivileges=yes` makes the kernel refuse `sudo` and renders the right
unusable (see [`FAQ.md`](../../FAQ.md)). The node now detects that **before**
running `sudo`: `can_update` is false and the refusal names the cause and the
fix, instead of relaying a `sudo` message about a kernel flag. The sudoers file
is **validated by `visudo` before being installed**: a file that does not parse
can break `sudo` for the whole machine.

Without that right, `update` falls back on `sudo -n <manager>` — which, on a
cleanly installed node (a service account with no sudo rule), fails. The console
therefore shows `update_granted`: "the right is there" or "we are counting on a
passwordless sudo" are two different things.

### `shell` — attended, so typing `sudo` is enough

The remote shell runs on a pty of which it is the **controlling terminal**
(`TIOCSCTTY` ioctl). Without that, the session has no terminal at all:
`/dev/tty` does not open, `sudo` refuses to ask for a password ("a terminal is
required") and job control is off. That used to be the case; it no longer is.

The consequence: to become root in a fleet shell you type `sudo`, and **a human
who knows the password types it**. There is no permanent credential-free root
access, and nothing to grant in advance.

In the browser, the panel is a real terminal: keystrokes go out **raw** (Ctrl-C,
Tab, arrows, Ctrl-D), nothing is echoed locally, and a small emulator written
for the occasion interprets the escape sequences. That is what makes a password
prompt correct: the remote pty turns echo off, and there is nothing on this side
to display it — where a line-by-line text field would have shown it in the
clear.

### An update's progress

An update takes minutes. Every step is announced **before** it runs (progress
that appears only once a step is finished is a summary, not progress):
`step 1/2: apt-get update`, then the real output, then the result with the
elapsed time. The node list shows a bar and the current step, and keeps the last
outcome once it is done.

## Seeing a node as the console sees it

Every card — a managed node, or a node that manages this one — carries a
**Details** button opening the shared node card in fleet's sheet: the real link,
its addresses, the latency, and what each side is allowed to do to the other. It
is the same view as in the console and in chat
(`src/webassets/nodeview.py`), mounted here rather than copied.

The "Message" button is there; the fleet buttons are not, since you are already
inside fleet.

## What you do with a managed node

- **Status** — uptime, load, memory, disks, distro, package manager. The last
  status is cached for display.
- **Update** — the node derives its plan **itself** from its own facts
  (`fleet_host.update_argv`): apt, dnf, yum, pacman, zypper, apk, xbps, brew,
  pkg, with `sudo -n`/`doas` if it is not root. Every command is an **argv
  list** from a fixed table — nothing the operator sends enters it, so there is
  no injection surface. A machine with no known manager, or no route to root,
  **refuses upfront** rather than starting something it cannot finish. The
  output is streamed live.
- **Shell** — a real pty. Bounds: `MAX_SHELLS = 4` at once per node, inactivity
  `SHELL_IDLE_TIMEOUT` (15 min) → reaped, input capped, SIGHUP then kill on
  close. The shell's environment is **cleaned**: the connector token living in
  `os.environ` is never passed to a child.
- **LAN scan** / **Provisioning** — below.

Machine facts are detected **when the app is installed** (first start) and
cached; `refresh_facts()` runs it again.

## LAN discovery and provisioning

The **Discover & deploy** section. The scan can run **locally** or on any
managed node holding `scan` — which is what makes deploying onto a remote site's
LAN possible.

### Phase 1 — discovering

The scan covers **every network the node is attached to**, at the prefix
actually in use — not an assumed `/24` around an address. Interfaces and their
masks are enumerated by `src.ip_utils.local_networks()`, which tries several
sources in order of reliability and falls through until one answers:

1. `/proc/net/route` (Linux) — the **connected** routes (with no gateway) are by
   definition the attached networks, with their exact mask;
2. `ioctl SIOCGIFADDR`/`SIOCGIFNETMASK` on each interface from
   `socket.if_nameindex()`;
3. the output of `ip -o -4 addr` or `ifconfig -a` (macOS, BSD);
4. only as a last resort, the old assumed `/24` around each known address —
   recognisable in that no interface name is attached to it.

A machine we cannot introspect therefore yields **fewer networks**, never an
exception.

What is set aside, and why:

| Set aside | Reason |
|---|---|
| IPv6 | scanning a /64 does not exist |
| loopback, link-local (`169.254/16`) | these are not LANs to discover |
| public prefixes | scanning a public prefix you happen to sit in means scanning strangers |
| `/31`, `/32` | no hosts to discover (point-to-point links) |

**Networks that are too large.** A `/16` is 65,000 connections: neither a LAN
discovery nor something you start by accident. A network beyond
`MAX_NET_ADDRESSES` (4096) is therefore **narrowed around our own address**
rather than abandoned, and the narrowing is **displayed** (`narrowed from
10.0.0.0/16`) so the operator can deliberately widen it by typing the prefix
they want into the *Subnets* field. When there is nothing to centre the slice on
(the local address is unknown), the network is skipped openly rather than
scanned in an arbitrary corner.

The scan itself: bounded TCP, the SSH banner read without sending anything.
Bounds: `MAX_HOSTS = 1024` in total, `MAX_SUBNETS = 16` networks,
`MAX_CONCURRENCY = 128`, 1.5 s per host, the banner capped. A `/8` given
**explicitly** is still refused.

### Targeting one precise machine

The *Targets* field accepts four forms, so that "scan my LAN" and "look at
exactly this machine" go through the same place:

| Input | Effect |
|---|---|
| *(empty)* | scans every attached network (automatic detection) |
| `192.168.1.0/24` | scans that subnet |
| `192.168.1.42` | **one machine only**, default SSH port |
| `192.168.1.42:2222` | one machine, explicit port |
| `nas.lan` / `nas.lan:2222` | one machine by name |
| `[fd00::5]:2222` | one machine over IPv6 |

Names are resolved **up front**, not left to `open_connection`: that would
resolve on asyncio's default executor, which is joined at shutdown
(`gotchas.md` §2). The bounded resolver lives in `ip_utils.bounded_getaddrinfo`,
shared with the STUN client.

Naming a machine is **not** scanning: a precise host typed by hand is allowed
wherever it is, including outside the private ranges and over IPv6 — it is a
connection to a machine the operator pointed at. A public **prefix**, on the
other hand, stays refused whoever typed it: scanning a public range means
scanning strangers.

What is not understood (a port out of bounds, a name that does not resolve, a
refused prefix) comes back **named** in `rejected`, shown below the results and
logged: a typo reads as a typo, never as "nothing found".

Left empty, automatic detection applies. The networks actually scanned (with
their interface, and where applicable the `narrowed` note) are shown above the
results and repeated in the log — "nothing found" is never ambiguous about
*where* we looked.

For each host found, `ssh-keyscan` fetches the **public host keys** and their
SHA-256 fingerprints, shown in the list. That pass is **concurrent and under a
global deadline** (`KEYSCAN_TIMEOUT`): doing it host by host with only a
per-host timeout left a busy LAN scan motionless for several minutes after the
sweep finished — seen from a remote node, that reads as "the scan did nothing".
Hosts not reached keep an empty key list, which the interface already shows as
"no host key".

### Phase 2 — choosing and deploying

The user ticks the machines, gives an SSH user, and **a password, a key, or
both** (both are tried). Local private keys are detected in `~/.ssh` —
**metadata only** (path, encrypted or not, the `.pub` comment); key material
never enters the app's state, it is `ssh -i` that reads the file at connection
time.

#### Giving a key to a containerised node

A container has no `~/.ssh`: detection finds nothing. Two routes, your choice,
described in [`docker/docker-compose.yml`](../../docker/docker-compose.yml):

1. **Mount your keys read-only** (`- ${HOME}/.ssh:/root/.ssh:ro`, or a single
   key). Read-only on purpose: the node only reads a key.
2. **Import a key from the console** — *Fleet → Discover & deploy → "Upload a
   key…"*. The block is visible **without scanning first**, since giving your
   key comes before looking for machines.

An imported key is put in the app's **encrypted drawer** (AES-256-GCM under a
key derived from the node's identity — the same trust boundary as the identity
file), one drawer entry per key so the state blob stays small. What the
interface reads (`/api/fleet/keys`) **never** contains the material: an
identifier, a name, "encrypted or not", the origin.

`ssh -i` wants a path, so an imported key does have to touch a filesystem at
some point. The window is **one command**: a 0700 temporary directory, a 0600
file, deleted on the way out (`MaterialisedKey`, the same shape as
`KnownHosts`). That is this feature's accepted trade-off, and it is bounded.

When provisioning runs on a **remote** node, an imported key has no path over
there: its **material** travels, in the same end-to-end encrypted payload as the
password, to a node already enrolled with `provision`, and is wiped there at the
end of the run. A key from disk travels instead as its mere path.

The Docker image ships `openssh-client`: without it the `provision` capability
declares itself unavailable, which is correct but useless for an image whose
whole role that is.

#### Elevation: stated, never guessed

The interface asks explicitly for:

- the login user, and **a password, a key, or both**;
- a checkbox **"this user can `sudo`"**;
- if it is unticked, **an account that can** and its password;
- **where NMesh goes**: *system* (`/opt/nmesh`, a dedicated service account —
  recommended) or *user* (the login account's home, no root).

Nothing is probed: testing whether an account can `sudo` leaves `sudo` failures
in the target's authentication log, and a machine that logs them is right to do
so. A *system* install with no way to reach root is refused **before** a session
is opened, not after the operator has typed everything.

A *user* install runs the node under the login account: everything that account
starts can then read its identity key. That is the trade-off to take when root
is not available, and the interface says so.

#### Two SSH sessions, for a precise reason

1. **Delivery** — a **self-extracting bootstrap** (`/bin/sh`, pure POSIX) is
   pushed on the standard input of `sh -s`. It asks for no privilege, so it
   cannot want a terminal. It verifies the payload's **SHA-256** **before
   writing a single file**, unpacks into a staging directory with an
   **unpredictable name** under the login account's home (so a hostile local
   user cannot pre-create it), and writes the **pre-authorisation** there in
   0600.
2. **Installation** — a second session **with a remote terminal** (`ssh -tt`).
   That is what lets `sudo` and `su` ask for their password *there*, on a
   terminal, and the local pty answer it — the same mechanism that answers
   OpenSSH. In other words: **the elevation secret never enters a script**,
   exactly like the SSH password. That is the entire reason for the split.

That second phase then **calls `./install.sh`** from the delivered tree, and
installs nothing itself. Dependencies, the dedicated service account, file
modes, the startup service, the liboqs cache, the configuration file: all of it
comes from `install.sh`. **A second, weaker installer is exactly how a remote
deployment ends up less solid than a local one** — that was the case, and it no
longer is.

The consequence: `install.sh` travels in the payload, and a tree without it is
refused when the payload is built.

Every step emits a `::step::` marker that the console turns into progress in the
**Activity** section. On failure, the **last lines the machine actually wrote**
(bounded) also go into the log: "dependency setup failed" with nothing behind it
leads nowhere for whoever has to fix it. A machine that refuses to be
provisioned is a **result**, not an exception: the batch carries on.

### Phase 3 — taking over trust

The goal is for the new machine to end up trusting the operator — with no human
to click "accept" on a box with no screen.

Two distinct things happen here, and it is worth not confusing them:

- **membership of the network** — a signed certificate, a place in the mesh;
- **the management relationship** — who this machine will *obey*.

**The certificate is signed by the node that ran the scan.** It is that node
which issues a **fresh, single-use invitation, one per machine** (never shared:
one failing must not burn another's), and writes it into the pre-authorisation
along with its own URIs. On first start, the new machine joins the mesh through
that invitation: it is the ordinary **invitation → handshake → `issue_cert`**
flow, simply automated. The certificate issued then chains up to the network's
root.

It is also the right node topologically: it is on the **same LAN** as the
machine being installed, and therefore reachable, where the operator may be
behind a NAT on the other side of the world.

> **The invitation's window.** A code typed by hand lives 5 minutes. This one is
> redeemed only **after** the dependencies are installed, which can take tens of
> minutes on a small machine: `generate_invite(ttl)` therefore allows a longer
> window (3 h at provisioning, bounded by `invite._MAX_TTL`). It stays
> **single-use**, delivered to a single machine over an authenticated SSH
> channel, and the anti-bruteforce lockout applies unchanged.

The pre-authorisation carries the **operator's node id and public key**
(delivered over the authenticated SSH channel, which is that side's root of
trust) plus a **single-use token**. On first start, the node:

1. joins the mesh through the invitation, and **waits for the session** — a
   `join` that returns proves nothing, the handshake that delivers the
   certificate comes after;
2. enrols the operator with the intended capabilities;
3. proves possession of the token, which lets the operator **bind this brand-new
   mesh identity to the provisioning run** they just did;
4. **deletes the file**, whatever happens.

If joining fails, the machine claims nothing and says so: claiming would go
nowhere with no route to anyone, and a "provisioned" machine that is on no
network must see itself as such. The provisioning result carries `joins: false`
when no invitation could be issued.

The token is not a long-term secret: it answers once to "is the node that just
appeared really the one I installed?". The operator keeps only
`sha256(token)`, and a token claimed twice finds nothing any more.

The operator's `node id` must derive from the key delivered with it, or the file
is rejected — the same invariant as everywhere else.

## SSH credential security

This is the only place in NMesh that holds a **foreign** credential. One rule:

> A credential lives **in memory**, for the length of a run, and **nowhere
> else**.

- Not on disk, not in `argv` (which `ps` shows to every local user), not in the
  environment (`/proc/<pid>/environ`), not in a log line, not in the app's
  drawer, not in a mesh packet in the clear.
- The mechanism: a **pseudo-terminal**. OpenSSH reads a password only from its
  controlling terminal; we give it one, write the secret into it at the prompt,
  and close it again.
- Each kind of prompt is answered **once only**: a re-prompt means the secret
  was wrong, and replaying it would only burn attempts against a lockout.
- `SshCredentials` has no `__dict__` (`__slots__`) and its `repr` is redacted: an
  accidental `print` or a traceback rendering its locals leaks nothing. `wipe()`
  drops the references as soon as the run ends (CPython `str` being immutable,
  the honest bound is "not kept", not "erased from RAM").
- When **no** host key could be confirmed (no `ssh-keyscan` on the scanning
  machine, or a silent target), the run falls back on `accept-new` — a real
  weakening, therefore **announced**: a `warning: no host key to pin` step
  appears in the log and the result carries `pinned: false`. Never silent.
- Host keys are **not** accepted blindly: the scan shows the fingerprints, the
  operator confirms, and the run pins exactly those in a throwaway `known_hosts`
  (0600, deleted afterwards) with `StrictHostKeyChecking=yes`. That is TOFU with
  a human in the loop, not an `accept-new` that signs for any interceptor.
- `IdentityAgent=none` + `IdentitiesOnly=yes`: what authenticates is what the
  operator chose, not what the agent happens to contain.

**When provisioning runs on a remote node**, the credential crosses the mesh
inside the **end-to-end encrypted** DATA payload, to a node the operator
explicitly enrolled with `provision`, and is wiped there as soon as the run
ends. That trust is already total — `provision` means the remote node runs code
we send it — so it adds no exposure the enrolment did not already grant. It is
an explicit choice, not an oversight.

### Why OpenSSH rather than an SSH stack in Python

Rewriting SSH means rewriting protocol cryptography (key exchange, host-key
verification, channel multiplexing) on the path that provisions new machines.
The charter puts security first and prefers not to invent crypto: the system's
`ssh` binary is audited, ubiquitous, and adds **no** Python dependency. The cost
is a runtime requirement (an `ssh` client on the provisioning machine), which we
**detect and report** instead of turning it into a silent weakness.

## Installing / enabling a built-in app

The registry: [`src/app_registry.py`](../../src/app_registry.py). Two distinct
states, because they do not mean the same thing:

- **installed** — this node keeps the app's state. **Uninstalling purges its
  encrypted drawer** (ledger, history). That is real and irreversible, not a
  flag.
- **enabled** — the app is plugged into the mesh right now. Disabling stops it
  and closes its section; its state survives, so re-enabling picks up where you
  were.

From the console: the **Apps** section, the Install / Enable / Disable /
Uninstall buttons. On the command line: `--fleet`, `--no-chat`. The choice is
persisted in `<data>/apps.json` (no secrets: names and two booleans; a corrupt
file gives back the defaults, never an app enabled that the operator did not
enable).

## Protocol (inside the `fleet` section of the DATA plane)

```
a signed request : type(1) ‖ alen(2) ‖ assertion ‖ json_body
a reply          : type(1) ‖ json_body
the shell stream : type(1) ‖ sid(16) ‖ raw bytes
```

| Type | Val | Direction | Signed |
|---|---|---|---|
| `ENROL_REQUEST` | 0x01 | operator → node | ✅ `fleet.enrol` |
| `ENROL_GRANT` / `_DENY` / `_REVOKE` | 0x02–0x04 | ↔ | ✅ `fleet.grant` |
| `PREAUTH_CLAIM` | 0x05 | new node → operator | ✅ `fleet.preauth` |
| `ENROL_NARROW` | 0x06 | operator → node (handing a right back) | ✅ `fleet.narrow` |
| `STATUS_REQUEST` / `_REPORT` | 0x10 / 0x11 | → / ← | the request only |
| `ERROR` | 0x12 | ← | — |
| `UPDATE_REQUEST` / `_OUTPUT` / `_RESULT` | 0x20–0x22 | → / ← / ← | the request only |
| `SHELL_OPEN` / `_OPENED` | 0x30 / 0x31 | → / ← | the opening only |
| `SHELL_INPUT` / `_OUTPUT` / `_RESIZE` / `_CLOSE` | 0x32–0x35 | ↔ | bound to the `sid` |
| `SCAN_REQUEST` / `_RESULT` | 0x40 / 0x41 | → / ← | the request only |
| `PROVISION_REQUEST` / `_PROGRESS` / `_RESULT` | 0x42–0x44 | → / ← / ← | the request only |
| `CONSOLE_REQUEST` / `_REPLY` | 0x50 / 0x51 | → / ← | the request only (`fleet.manage`) |

**No new mesh packet type**: everything travels in `DATA`, section
`builtin_id("fleet")`. The node's management plane is untouched.

Replies are not signed: they take the already authenticated E2E channel, and are
accepted only if they answer a `rid` **we** issued, for that kind of action,
**from the node** we sent it to. An unsolicited reply is dropped.

## Bounds

| Constant | Value |
|---|---|
| `MAX_BODY` | 56,000 B per JSON frame |
| `MAX_SHELLS` | 4 shells hosted at once |
| `SHELL_IDLE_TIMEOUT` | 900 s |
| `SHELL_INPUT_MAX` | 8192 B per input frame |
| `MAX_INFLIGHT` | 64 requests tracked on the operator side |
| `MAX_OPERATORS` / `MAX_MANAGED` | 64 / 4096 |
| `MAX_PENDING` | 256 pending requests (TTL 7 days) |
| `UPDATE_TIMEOUT` / `SCAN_TIMEOUT` | 1800 s / 300 s |
| `MAX_PAYLOAD` | 16 MB pushed at provisioning |
| `MAX_SSH_KEYS` / `MAX_SSH_KEY_BYTES` | 8 imported keys / 64 KB per key |
| `MAX_SUBNETS` / `MAX_NET_ADDRESSES` | 16 networks scanned / 4096 addresses per network before narrowing |

The ledger **fails closed**: a corrupt or tampered blob yields an **empty**
ledger (we forget who we believed, rather than trusting a forged list), and an
unknown capability name is dropped at load — a doctored file cannot slip a
capability through.

## Tracking operations

An action sent to a remote node answers **asynchronously**: the HTTP request
returns at once, the answer arrives later over the mesh. The console bridge
therefore keeps a register of operations (`jobs` in the snapshot): `rid`, type,
the node targeted, state (`running` / `ok` / `failed`) and a detail. The
deployment panel follows its own instead of staying frozen on "in progress".

The interface presents them in the **notification centre** — the bell in the top
bar, with an unread counter. Pending access requests are there too: those are
the two things that need somebody's attention. An item is "unread" by its `rid`
**and** its state, so an operation finishing is news even if its start already
was; opening the menu marks what is shown. Clicking a row goes to the section
concerned.

> These items used to be badges placed directly in the top bar, which **grew**
> with every operation — the one place on the page whose height must not move.
> The list is bounded (`NOTIF_MAX`), and so is the set of read keys
> (`NOTIF_SEEN_MAX`).

Two properties not to break:

- **Closing does not depend on ordering.** A nearby peer can answer in under a
  millisecond, before the calling thread has noted the `rid`. A closure that
  arrives too early is set aside and applied to the record, or the operation
  would stay "in progress" forever.
- **Everything is bounded** (`MAX_JOBS`), including the table of early closures.

## Reply size

A reply must fit in **one DATA frame**. Cutting JSON at a byte offset produces
something the recipient cannot parse and silently drops — the worst possible
failure for a reply an operator is waiting on. Replies are therefore trimmed by
**removing entries**, never bytes: `_dump_json(document, *lists_to_shrink)`
shortens the named lists until the frame fits, and `truncated` says how many
went. The interface reports it ("N result(s) dropped… narrow the target"). If
there is nothing left to remove, a readable error goes out instead of an
unreadable frame.

## Console API

All behind the console session (Bearer or cookie). Details:
[`Docs/WebConsole/guide`](../WebConsole/guide).

| Method | Route | Role |
|---|---|---|
| GET | `/api/fleet/state?since=` | snapshot (nodes, notifications, log, capabilities, facts, **jobs**, scans) |
| GET | `/api/fleet/shell?sid=&offset=` | terminal bytes (base64) |
| GET | `/api/fleet/keys` | local SSH keys — **metadata only** |
| POST | `/api/fleet/enrol` / `approve` / `deny` / `revoke` | enrolment |
| POST | `/api/fleet/caps-set` | an operator's rights **on this node** (a local human) |
| POST | `/api/fleet/caps-request` / `caps-drop` | ask for / hand back a right on a managed node |
| GET | `/api/remote/targets` | nodes that granted `manage`, and whether a session is open |
| POST | `/api/remote/connect` / `disconnect` | opens / closes a session on a managed node's console |
| *(any console route)* | the `X-NMesh-Node: <id>` header | relays the call to that node instead of running it here |
| POST | `/api/fleet/status` / `update` / `scan` | actions |
| POST | `/api/fleet/shell` / `input` / `resize` / `close` | the shell |
| POST | `/api/fleet/provision` | deployment |
| POST | `/api/apps/enable` / `disable` / `install` / `uninstall` | built-in apps |

